From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Fri, 04 Sep 2026 14:07:44 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] virtio-media: drain only what poll() actually reports

virtio_media_host_readable() had no idea which condition woke it, because
qemu_set_fd_handler() does not pass one: util/aio-posix.c only arms
G_IO_IN|G_IO_HUP|G_IO_ERR for a read handler and G_IO_OUT|G_IO_ERR for a
write handler, and this device registers the same function as both. So the
drain issued VIDIOC_DQEVENT until it failed and then VIDIOC_DQBUF on both
queues until they failed, every single time.

The comment in that function argued this cannot busy-loop, because vb2 only
raises POLLOUT while the output done_list is non-empty and the drain empties
it. Measurement disagrees. strace of the device model during 360p H.264
hardware-decoded playback on RPi4 (2026-09-04, 8 s, ~128 frames):

  VIDIOC_DQBUF   3818 calls, 96 % EAGAIN   = 30 per frame
  VIDIOC_DQBUF   2675 calls, 95 % EAGAIN   = 21 per frame
  VIDIOC_DQEVENT 2005 calls, 100 % ENOENT  = 16 per frame
  VIDIOC_DQEVENT 1300 calls, 100 % ENOENT  = 10 per frame
  VIDIOC_QBUF     248 calls (the real work) =  2 per frame

77 wasted ioctls per frame against 2 useful ones, and 1299 of 1835 ppoll()
calls came back with a zero timeout, i.e. the main loop never slept. On Xen
that main loop is also what completes ioreqs, so the waste directly delays
every DomA vCPU that touches MMIO.

Three separate reasons: DQEVENT can only ever succeed when POLLPRI is set and
POLLPRI was not even being watched; a wake-up caused by one queue also probed
the other; and vb2 raises POLLERR for a queue with no buffers, which the read
handler's G_IO_ERR picks up, producing a wake-up where nothing at all is
ready.

So establish the real condition with one poll(fd, ..., 0) and issue only the
matching ioctls: DQEVENT for POLLPRI, the output queue for POLLOUT and the
capture queue for POLLIN, per v4l2_m2m_poll()'s own definition (src done_list
-> EPOLLOUT, dst done_list -> EPOLLIN). virtio_media_queue_index() maps
output to 0 and everything else to 1, which is the same indexing.

Verified with a standalone m2m poll test, which drives a queue the way this
device does and counts both strategies against the same state, on vicodec
(same v4l2_m2m_fop_poll implementation) and on the real bcm2835-codec-decode
node:

                polls   ioctls now   ioctls gated   saved
  vicodec        8410        25230           8410   66.7 %
  bcm2835        1810         5430           1810   66.7 %

The test also shows the level is trustworthy: "POLLOUT set but the output
queue was empty" was 0/8410 and 0/1810, and every DQBUF that would have
succeeded had its bit set, so gating drops nothing. It reproduces the POLLERR
behaviour too (poll before REQBUFS and after STREAMOFF both return POLLERR).

The extra poll() adds no new vb2_poll() side effect -- the main loop already
polls this fd the same way every iteration -- and the fileio-emulation trap
described in virtio_media_do_open() cannot trigger here because the handler
is only registered while a queue is streaming, which means it has buffers.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [depends on the downstream virtio-media device
 (0216), which upstream qemu does not have]
diff --git a/hw/virtio/virtio-media.c b/hw/virtio/virtio-media.c
index c4e2f1d2dba..b7d9ad88e01 100644
--- a/hw/virtio/virtio-media.c
+++ b/hw/virtio/virtio-media.c
@@ -294,13 +294,9 @@ static void virtio_media_send_error_event(VirtIOMediaSession *s, int err)
  * before allocating capture buffers. A decoder with no event path stalls at the
  * first frame with nothing in the log to say why.
  */
-static void virtio_media_host_readable(void *opaque)
+/* Called only when POLLPRI is set. Drains every pending V4L2 event. */
+static void virtio_media_drain_events(VirtIOMediaSession *s)
 {
-    VirtIOMediaSession *s = opaque;
-    VirtIOMedia *vm = s->vm;
-    int i;
-
-    /* V4L2 events first: a SOURCE_CHANGE tells the guest to re-negotiate. */
     for (;;) {
         struct virtio_media_event_event evt = {
             .hdr = { .event = VIRTIO_MEDIA_EVT_EVENT, .session_id = s->id },
@@ -309,16 +305,94 @@ static void virtio_media_host_readable(void *opaque)
         if (ioctl(s->fd, VIDIOC_DQEVENT, &evt.event) < 0) {
             break;
         }
-        virtio_media_send_event(vm, &evt, sizeof(evt));
+        virtio_media_send_event(s->vm, &evt, sizeof(evt));
     }
+}
+
+static void virtio_media_host_readable(void *opaque)
+{
+    VirtIOMediaSession *s = opaque;
+    VirtIOMedia *vm = s->vm;
+    struct pollfd pfd = { .fd = s->fd, .events = POLLIN | POLLOUT | POLLPRI };
+    short revents;
+    int i;
 
-    /* Then completed buffers, on both queues. */
+    /*
+     * NOTE: establish with one poll what actually happened, and issue only
+     * the ioctls that correspond to it.
+     *
+     * qemu_set_fd_handler() does not tell the callback which event woke it
+     * (util/aio-posix.c:158 only arms G_IO_IN|G_IO_HUP|G_IO_ERR for read and
+     * G_IO_OUT|G_IO_ERR for write). A blind drain -- DQEVENT until EAGAIN plus
+     * DQBUF until EAGAIN on both queues, every time -- is not justified by the
+     * argument that "vb2 only raises POLLOUT while done_list is non-empty, so
+     * draining clears the level and it cannot spin": measurement disagrees
+     * with that.
+     *
+     * strace of qemu during HW-decoded playback on RPi4 (2026-09-04, 8 s, ~128 frames):
+     *
+     *   VIDIOC_DQBUF   3818 calls, 96 % EAGAIN        = 30 per frame
+     *   VIDIOC_DQBUF   2675 calls, 95 % EAGAIN        = 21 per frame
+     *   VIDIOC_DQEVENT 2005 calls, 100 % ENOENT       = 16 per frame
+     *   VIDIOC_DQEVENT 1300 calls, 100 % ENOENT       = 10 per frame
+     *   VIDIOC_QBUF     248 calls, useful work        =  2 per frame
+     *
+     * = 77 wasted calls per frame; the useful QBUFs are just 2.
+     *
+     * Three reasons. (a) DQEVENT always fails unless POLLPRI is set, yet
+     * POLLPRI is not even watched. (b) Whichever queue caused the wakeup,
+     * DQBUF is issued on the other one too. (c) vb2 **raises POLLERR on a
+     * queue that holds no buffers**, and since qemu arms G_IO_ERR this
+     * function is called for POLLERR too, where again everything misses.
+     *
+     * A standalone m2m poll test measured both vicodec and the board's
+     * bcm2835-codec-decode:
+     *
+     *                 polls   ioctls now   with revents        saved
+     *   vicodec        8410        25230           8410       66.7 %
+     *   bcm2835        1810         5430           1810       66.7 %
+     *
+     * The test also confirms (c): the poll before REQBUFS and the poll after
+     * STREAMOFF both return revents=POLLERR. And "POLLOUT set but nothing on
+     * OUTPUT" was 0/8410 and 0/1810 = **the level does clear correctly**, so
+     * the bits that are set can be trusted as they are. Nothing is missed
+     * either: 0 (every successful DQBUF had its matching bit set).
+     *
+     * On the extra poll(): the main loop already polls this fd the same way
+     * every iteration, so the side effect of passing through vb2_poll() (the
+     * fileio emulation noted in the virtio_media_do_open() comment) is not
+     * increased. And this function is registered only while a queue is
+     * streaming, so it never hits fileio's trigger (a queue with no buffers).
+     */
+    if (poll(&pfd, 1, 0) <= 0) {
+        return;
+    }
+    revents = pfd.revents;
+
+    /* V4L2 events first: a SOURCE_CHANGE tells the guest to re-negotiate. */
+    if (revents & POLLPRI) {
+        virtio_media_drain_events(s);
+    }
+
+    /*
+     * Then completed buffers, on the queue poll() actually flagged.
+     *
+     * The mapping exactly as v4l2_m2m_poll() defines it:
+     *   src (= OUTPUT, index 0) done_list not empty -> EPOLLOUT
+     *   dst (= CAPTURE, index 1) done_list not empty -> EPOLLIN
+     * virtio_media_queue_index() maps OUTPUT to 0 and everything else to 1,
+     * so it agrees with this index.
+     */
     for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(s->queues); i++) {
         VirtIOMediaQueue *q = &s->queues[i];
+        const short want = (i == 0) ? POLLOUT : POLLIN;
 
         if (!q->streaming || q->num_bufs == 0) {
             continue;
         }
+        if (!(revents & want)) {
+            continue;
+        }
         for (;;) {
             struct virtio_media_event_dqbuf evt = {
                 .hdr = { .event = VIRTIO_MEDIA_EVT_DQBUF, .session_id = s->id },
